WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:13.410
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I am not going to make any organist comments,

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.439
so we will move on quickly to the annual report and accounts from Karen.

00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:22.539
&gt;&gt;Karen wheeler: Not much to say, generally.
The annual report was published and laid before

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:31.869
parliament on 21st July, and I hope people
have the opportunity to read it. I do want

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:38.930
to thank everybody who contributed to it;
it is very much a whole organisation exercise,

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:46.710
and I think it is a good reflection of the
work that we have been doing over the last

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:52.200
year. This will be presented to our AGM in
October as well.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:58.740
I did just want to highlight one thing that
was reported in the annual report, which relates

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:08.960
to a serious incident which we were alerted
to at the end of March by one of our contracted

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:17.670
suppliers at the time - SBS, Shared Business
Services - who raised with us an issue that

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:26.229
they had identified of a large volume of correspondence
which had not been processed. This correspondence

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:35.430
generally is from, for example, providers
who are sending correspondence to a GP but

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:42.960
it goes to the wrong GP, or that GP does not
have the relevant patient, and therefore it

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.900
needs to get forwarded to the right GP.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:56.260
So this volume of correspondence, which had
become a backlog over a period of years, had

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:01.729
grown up into a very large number and was
reported to us at the end of March. We immediately

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:09.729
established a national incident team to handle
this incident, and to process and review and

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:16.560
handle the correspondence, and to ensure that
any aspects that might affect patients are

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:25.010
being followed up. That work continues, but
letters have been written out to any and every

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:31.850
GP practice where there has been identified
a potential risk to patients, and we are in

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:40.070
the process of following that up. Those numbers
are relatively contained.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:46.030
The thing I wanted to bring to the attention
of the board, really, was the fact that we

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:55.210
are not yet happy with the information and
the assurance that we have from SBS about

00:02:55.210 --> 00:03:04.480
how this incident arose, and the potential
extent of the correspondence that might be

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:11.920
at issue. So we will obviously be taking this
up very strongly with the Department of Health,

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.610
who sponsor this organisation, as well as
with SBS itself. I just wanted to ensure that

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:22.530
everyone was sighted on that issue.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.860
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Karen. Would you
wish to add anything, Simon?

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:31.599
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: No; only to say the Audit
Committee has also, under Karen's direction,

00:03:31.599 --> 00:03:38.830
reviewed this, and I think the NHS England
incident team that Karen has stood up is pulling

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:47.040
out all the stops to sort through this situation.
However, to be blunt about it, the information

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:53.950
available to us is that the response from
SBS has been unsatisfactory and obfuscatory,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:58.260
and we intend to formally bring that to the
attention of the Department of Health as the

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.010
co shareholder in SBS.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:08.920
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: The Audit Committee is all
over it, and clearly looking not just at managing

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:17.250
the incident but also the lessons learned,
and how we can make sure that our processes

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:23.510
and information base are in place to get ahead
of these things as well.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:28.210
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: It might be worth me just
clarifying: the service that this relates

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:34.680
to is not a service that we carry out any
more; we decided that actually it was not

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:41.770
an appropriate service for us to provide,
so indeed when such correspondence is surfaced

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.919
it is now sent back to the original provider,
so that they can update their records and

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:53.580
send it to the right GP's surgery. So that
service has been closed down as part of the

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.580
process.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.230
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: These were a whole set of
arrangements that NHS England inherited on

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:02.310
its inception; these were not agreements that
were ever negotiated by NHS England itself.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:13.300
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Indeed; these stem from contracts
that pre dated NHS England from PCT days,

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:18.280
and are a legacy of those arrangements. In
fact, the arrangements that we have been putting

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:25.190
in place around the primary care support services
contract is what has surfaced this issue.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:33.250
So I think we can be confident and assured
that this type of activity is not going to

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:37.190
arise in the contract that we currently operate.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:40.150
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Maybe, Malcolm, it is also
worth just Karen saying a sentence then too

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:46.570
about the work that you have to ensure that,
in the transition of these arrangements from

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:52.259
SBS, we are holding the new contractor's feet
to the fire as well, given the initial teething

00:05:52.259 --> 00:05:54.419
issues that arose there from a GPs' point
of view.

00:05:54.419 --> 00:05:58.850
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Yes, very definitely. So
as I say, on this particular service about

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:09.680
handling of GP correspondence, that was closed
down. As the board knows, we worked to procure

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:17.039
a new owner for managing the service around
primary care support services, and successfully

00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:25.960
transitioned those services to Capita. For
the first six months of the contract, that

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:32.900
service ran very successfully, but at the
point in March when some very significant

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:38.979
transformations were happening - very worthwhile
and positive transformations, intended to

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:48.200
improve the services, and I think were going
to be welcomed by our stakeholders: GP practices

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:59.110
and other primary care contractors - we have
hit some problems associated with the implementation

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:07.699
of those transformation changes, and as a
result there have been significant issues

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:13.850
which have affected stakeholders, GP surgeries
and other primary care contractors.

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:21.500
That has been hugely unwelcome and obviously
something that we are very disappointed with,

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:28.440
both ourselves as managing the Capita contractor
but also Capita themselves are hugely disappointed.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:34.580
We are working extremely hard to recover those
service issues, and have been talking to the

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:45.030
BMA and GPC and stakeholders constantly through
this process. I am confident that the service

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:54.330
is in recovery, and even now many of the backlogs
that did grow up in the early months have

00:07:54.330 --> 00:08:02.240
been recovered and eliminated, and service
is improving all the time. There is more work,

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:07.960
though, to be done around that, and I am working
very closely with Capita to make sure that

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:14.979
that gets delivered as quickly as possible,
and we produce the service that our stakeholders

00:08:14.979 --> 00:08:17.599
and contractors have a right to expect.

00:08:17.599 --> 00:08:22.720
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Okay, thank you very much,
Karen. I think that point about Capita reinforces

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:29.020
the point that David has made about our need
to get on top of managing third party contracts

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:30.300
in this space.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:35.930
I think so far as the SBS experience is concerned,
the critical thing to note is that this is

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:40.779
not just bureaucratic paper moving in the
back office; there were instances which may

00:08:40.779 --> 00:08:48.149
have real implications for patients, and it
would be foolhardy for us not to take this

00:08:48.149 --> 00:08:53.920
immensely seriously and to take the steps
that the Chief Executive has proposed. Thank

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.220
you very much for that report.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:09:01.610
May we then move on to the next item, which
is the NHS customer contact and complaints

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:03.600
annual report?

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:13.829
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: This is an annual report
of the performance against handling contacts

00:09:13.829 --> 00:09:24.079
and complaints. This report is slightly different
this year, so I will go into the details of

00:09:24.079 --> 00:09:31.769
it. There is some positive news here in terms
of the positive improvements that have been

00:09:31.769 --> 00:09:37.709
made to the service that we provide, and the
complaints that we are talking about and contacts.

00:09:37.709 --> 00:09:43.269
The contacts and general enquiries come from
many, many different sources; the complaints

00:09:43.269 --> 00:09:53.079
are specifically associated with primary care
service, and that is probably where the complexity

00:09:53.079 --> 00:09:59.720
of the work is. We have very good service
and responsiveness in terms of handling contacts

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:06.510
and responding to them, and we have implemented
some positive changes associated with a new

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:09.639
system and a quality framework.

00:10:09.639 --> 00:10:14.750
Through the work of implementing our quality
framework, and working with our regional teams,

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:20.629
who are the teams who manage the complaints,
we identified during the course of the year

00:10:20.629 --> 00:10:30.589
that there was inconsistency in what was being
called a complaint and being logged as a complaint.

00:10:30.589 --> 00:10:37.230
I think it was very useful that we surfaced
this quite early, because what we discovered

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:47.019
was that some things were being categorised
as complaints which were not really valid

00:10:47.019 --> 00:10:53.809
complaints, on the basis that they were not
something that we could investigate, and indeed

00:10:53.809 --> 00:11:01.730
the patient who raised those complaints or
issues did not want us to investigate those

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:10.829
issues, but simply to record a concern. So
we have re categorised a number of these issues

00:11:10.829 --> 00:11:15.709
as 'concerns', and therefore we have much
greater clarity on the number therefore of

00:11:15.709 --> 00:11:24.940
formal complaints, that we can actually investigate
and resolve for the patient who raised them,

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:31.239
which is good. We have a much better handle
on what is going on in our service and our

00:11:31.239 --> 00:11:36.579
primary care service. That is good. The number
of actual complaints is much smaller than

00:11:36.579 --> 00:11:39.639
we were reporting last year.

00:11:39.639 --> 00:11:49.280
However, those complaints are complex, and
what we have actually done is stripped them

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:56.559
away from the reporting. Because the numbers
of concerns that are being addressed which

00:11:56.559 --> 00:12:05.509
are the less serious issues have got quite
good resolution: 70% of those within the target

00:12:05.509 --> 00:12:12.749
time for resolution. However, the complaints
which take longer and are more complex are

00:12:12.749 --> 00:12:18.899
ones where we only have, at the moment, a
46% success rate in resolving those within

00:12:18.899 --> 00:12:20.899
a 40 day period, which is our target.

00:12:20.899 --> 00:12:26.769
Now, there are a number of reasons for that
which we are getting under. We have certainly

00:12:26.769 --> 00:12:34.519
had issues in our regional teams associated
with staffing and contractor support associated

00:12:34.519 --> 00:12:42.989
with these services. These are also much more
complex. I think it is probably worth noting

00:12:42.989 --> 00:12:52.139
in the report that our customer satisfaction
associated with not only the resolution of

00:12:52.139 --> 00:12:59.149
complaints, but also the time to resolve those
complaints, is quite positive. It is 71% at

00:12:59.149 --> 00:13:05.509
the end of the year. So I think the most important
thing for patients who raise those complaints

00:13:05.509 --> 00:13:11.049
is that they get a satisfactory resolution
to the complaint.

00:13:11.049 --> 00:13:20.519
So we are doing a piece of work in the autumn
to review that service, the processes that

00:13:20.519 --> 00:13:27.299
we are going through to manage those complaints,
and whether we have the right degree of resources;

00:13:27.299 --> 00:13:36.379
and indeed, what the appropriate target is
for us to set ourselves so that we can make

00:13:36.379 --> 00:13:41.750
sure that we are hitting that target. If the
current target is the right target, we will

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:47.889
continue with that and see what we need to
do to improve it, but if that seems too ambitious

00:13:47.889 --> 00:13:52.689
given the complexity and the scale of those
complaints, then we may come back to the board

00:13:52.689 --> 00:13:58.459
and consider a re baselining of what that
target is.

00:13:58.459 --> 00:14:02.639
So that is a little bit more complicated than
normal, or I hope it will be. Now we have

00:14:02.639 --> 00:14:10.009
a good baseline, good data, and a better handle
on what is going on. I am confident we will

00:14:10.009 --> 00:14:14.290
be able to give you a much simpler story in
the future, and what we would hope to do is

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:19.279
probably consolidate this report with our
annual report, so that it just becomes part

00:14:19.279 --> 00:14:20.930
of our annual reporting process.

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:25.490
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you for bringing it
separately to our attention on this occasion.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:33.609
Are there any queries or comments to Karen
on that? No. Thank you very much. It has obviously

00:14:33.609 --> 00:14:38.489
been a sort of thermometer by which we can
gauge our performance, so critically important

00:14:38.489 --> 00:14:40.079
that we track it closely.

00:14:40.079 --> 00:14:46.850
We are now going to turn to the next section
of the agenda, which is around governance.

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:51.160
The first paper on this is the governance
manual approvals.

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:57.669
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: I do not think we want to
spend a huge amount of time on this.

00:14:57.669 --> 00:15:04.790
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: That may be the understatement
of the day!

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:13.670
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: This is a consolidation of
many pieces of governance work which we are

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:18.989
trying to bring and manage more effectively.
We have been through this in a lot of detail

00:15:18.989 --> 00:15:20.339
with our Audit and Risk Committee.

00:15:20.339 --> 00:15:24.480
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I do not think we should
repeat the work of the Audit and Risk Assurance

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:25.639
Committee. May we just take that?

00:15:25.639 --> 00:15:26.639
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: It is recommended.

00:15:26.639 --> 00:15:28.360
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Okay, so may we just take
that?

